feat(text): a new line without a new paragraph, and copying how writing looks (M103)

The soft break was already built. LineBreakAnalyzer has listed U+2028 among its
mandatory breaks since M1, TextLayoutEngine obeys them, and the editor's
sanitiser lets it through because it is not a control character. Every part
worked and no key put one in - the fourteenth instance of the audit's category
and the cheapest of them: a command, a keyboard row and a menu item.

text.lineBreak (Shift+Enter) is for an address, a list of names, a heading that
runs to two lines - where Enter starts a new paragraph and takes the paragraph
gap and first-line indent with it, so the committee has been living with
headings that are two paragraphs pretending to be one.

text.pickUpLook and text.putLookDown are the format painter, named for the job
rather than the tool. They carry a style name, not a bundle of attributes:
everything about how a run looks is already a named style applied by reference,
so the name carries font, size, colour, bold, italic and underline together and
putting it down is one command. The look is not forgotten after one use,
because making six headings match is six uses.

A wording bug the gesture exposed: Avalonia's Key.Enter is Key.Return and the
enum reports the older name, so the app would have advertised "Shift+Return"
for a key every keyboard in the lodge calls Enter.

The performance test gets its third and final version. Its reasoning was always
right - a wall-clock assertion on a contended machine measures the neighbours -
and its detection was wrong twice, first keying on CI, then counting sibling
test processes. It has stopped guessing: gross regressions by default, and
TRESTLEBOARD_PERF=1 asks for the real 16ms budget on an idle machine. The
precise gate was always the one that counts layout passes.

